A||||3||FieroPerformance||Rear brake caliper question...||||5||
Z||000000||FieroPerformance||06-26-2001||06:38 PM||||I am in the process of replacing the pads of my right rear brakes. I have the old pads off but I am unable to push the piston back up to fit the new ones on with the rotor in between them. I have tried a c clamp and a socket so that it is not pressuring the nut but it will not move. I have even tried disconnecting the brake line and I am still unable to move it. Anyone had this problem before?<P>Thanks,<BR>-Charlie||||reg||5
Z||000001||Steve Normington||06-26-2001||07:02 PM||||You have to disconnect the parking brake lever from the caliper before the piston will move back into the caliper. Or, buy a GM piston compressor tool (looks like a cube with square holes in each side) to rotate the piston back into the bore.||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||06-26-2001||07:54 PM||||There are 2 pistons used in the 84-87 caliper. <P>The ones with the rubber "plug" in the middle of the piston face definatly should not be rotated. Those you have to take the e brake arm off and press in. (Make sure the e brake arm moves first.)<P>I have to find a spare one of the those that does not have the plug in the piston. Those are built diferently. I think those can be rotated w/o problems but i've got no data on them. Even GM doesn't seem to provide any info by way of a TSB.||||reg||1
Z||000003||FieroPerformance||06-26-2001||09:43 PM||||That fixed it. Thanks a lot guys!||||reg||1
